INSERT INTO "reclamations" VALUES(83,'02/14/2019','Vehicle loan or lease','Loan','Struggling to pay your loan','I have a 2 party loan with [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]. There is a deceased party on the loan since [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]. The finance company was made aware of the issue, provided a death certificate in [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]. 

The company has policies and procedures that they follow in regards to deceased borrowers. They are stating that due to the deceased borrower on the account their Customer Assistance team is not able to process a deferment on my loan. 

With my knowledge of working in the auto loan industry, a deferment can be processed for any borrower listed on the contract of the loan. Even when there is a deceased borrower on the account. I only have to provide them with notification of the deceased borrower which was completed in [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] of [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]. The vehicle is not on a single party loan and therefore I do not have to submit Fidicuary documents. 

On XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ], I contacted the company and was denied the ability to process the extension due to the above scenario. I was advised that I would be contacted on [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] by a manager that would ensure that the situation was resolved. No follow up happened from the company. 

[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] I contacted the customer service department and information was collected, transferred to deceased handling that ensured me that I was eligible for the assistance. Transferred back to customer assistance team only to be denied of the assistance and the processing of the deferment due to the same error. 

I will now have my credit reporting hit for 30 days past due to unjust handling of my loan that is an error of the company. 
I feel that my loan is being discriminated against due to the deceased borrower and being handled with the fairness and consistency of other two party loans without a deceased borrower.','SANTANDER CONSUMER USA HOLDINGS INC.',2.0,2.0,'the finance company was made aware of the issue, provided a death certificate in [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] the company has policies and procedures that they follow in regards to');
INSERT INTO "reclamations" VALUES(84,'02/14/2019','Debt collection','Payday loan debt','Took or threatened to take negative or legal action','XX/XX/2019 A debt collector/ Lawyer ''s office started to call my place of employment months ago but more so in one week recently. The unidentified caller demanded payment and my personal financial information that very minute or else I will be sued as threatened in the past. I kindly asked for the calls to stop as I have before. That same week the same nameless person ( who refused to give me a name ) once again threatened of a lawsuit and stated I would be embarrassed at work for wage garnishments. Last call was a woman laughing and stating I will be in court and hung up .My place of employment has caller ID and all revealed the name of the same company and phone number. Clearly, this company does not honor the FDCPA rights. In addition, the main lawyer of this company has been in and out of practice due to violations of the California state bar. Now my lawyer needs to take action against this company.','D. SCOTT CARRUTHERS',2.0,2.0,'a debt collector/ Lawyer started to call my place of employment months ago. the unidentified caller demanded payment and my financial information. the same nameless person once again');
INSERT INTO "reclamations" VALUES(85,'02/14/2019','Credit card or prepaid card','Store credit card','Fees or interest','I pay my credit card bills on the day of the due date each month. This has never been an issue with any credit card providers until I signed up for the [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] store card by Synchrony Bank. Currently, if I pay this on the due date, I am assessed a {$25.00} late fee for doing so. This has happened twice to me now, as it appears to trigger a late fee if submitted on the due date, but after a certain time. For instance, if I pay it in the morning of the due date, there is no late fee. But if I pay it i the afternoon, there is a late fee. Both times are before their [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] EST cutoff. Typically, late fees are considered to be detrimental to one ''s overall credit score. How can Synchrony get away with charging late fees for on-time payments? It also leads to the question of why are they holding onto this late fee of {$25.00} before refunding it? What are they doing with that money that they essentially took from me without my permission? Are they conducting some sort of a short-term investment strategy with their clients money/late fees? 

I reached out to them to dispute an unwarranted late fee, and they kindly explained that it would be returned since I paid before the due date/time cutoff. They said their system automatically assessed the late fee, and it will be refunded. However, I am not buying that it is this innocent to say, " sorry, we took your money accidentally, and are returning it. '''' On top of that, I had asked if this affects my credit score, as any late fee would. They said they could not answer that question. I do not have proof that it has affected my credit score, but if it does, I will not be a happy customer. 

This is very poor practice, and to be honest I am surprised it is allowed by any credit card company. This needs to be investigated so that Synchrony is no longer able to take advantage of their customers and their money. If their internal software is not able to handle processing same day payments without late fees, then perhaps they should have their ability to operate suspended until they can resolve this.','SYNCHRONY FINANCIAL',2.0,2.0,'if I pay this on the day of the due date, I am assessed a $25.00 late fee. this has happened twice to me now, as it appears');
