INSERT INTO "reclamations" VALUES(223,'01/12/2019','Credit card or prepaid card','General-purpose credit card or charge card','Problem when making payments','I went to get gas yesterday with my [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] card ending in [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]. I was told that I could not make the purchase and I had to call the number on the card to find out why. 
Today I called [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] Customer service at [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]. First they could not find my account. Then they told me I hd the wrong account my account ended in [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] not [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]. I was then told that the reason that I could no longer make purchases was because I am due for XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] and XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] payments in the amount of [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]. ( its not even XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] yet? ).  I asked him when was the last payment sent he said sometime in XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] they received [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]. I checked with my bank while on the phone with [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] and found that a payment in the amount of [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] was sent on XX/XX/2018 and received by [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] on XX/XX/2018. I was told I needed to fax proof of that payment in. I said ok give me the fax number. The Representative would not give me the fax number and said my call needed to be transferred to a different department. 
I was switched to a gentleman that identified himself as [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] # [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]. I told him what happened and that I needed the fax number to show proof of payment. He said that as far as he was concerned that was not necessary as [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] never received the payment on XX/XX/2018. I was told I needed to call my bank [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] and they would have to handle it. He then went on to say I would have to pay the money. I told him to close my account. All this sounded strange so I called [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] My Bank the payer and spoke to [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] she told me that that was not the case the money had been paid to [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] and I needed to take it up with them. So now I am am writing you the CFPB. I need to find out what happened to the [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] that was taken out of my account and sent to [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] and why [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] never applied the payment. Please Help me resolve this issue. 
See attached my [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] card and my proof of payment to them from [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]  [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]','SYNCHRONY FINANCIAL',2.0,2.0,'a gentleman called me to get gas yesterday with my [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] card ending in [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]. ');
INSERT INTO "reclamations" VALUES(224,'01/12/2019','Checking or savings account','Checking account','Managing an account','On XX/XX/2019at [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ], we received a phone call. The caller ID displayed Bank of America ( photo attached ), so we answered the phone. The individual identified herself as a Bank of America ( BofA ) employee and explained that one of our debit cards had been compromised and she needed to order a new card and setup a new pin. I think she notice our hesitation to cooperate with her and she explained that the number we see on the caller ID was the same number on the back of our debit card. She also told us our address, it was correct. We did check, and the numbers matched. She did ask us if we made a purchase at a Florida [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]  store and we said no. She advised us to ignore all other texts and emails from BofA, because while we were on the phone she was resetting our account, I did get another text from [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] ( photo attached ) asking me to confirm the [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] purchase. She told us not to reply because she was taking care of the transaction. She continued to explain that she needed a code for our account to help us, she said she would send it to my cell phone and then gave us the number, it was correct ( she had our cell number ). Then we received 4 texts from [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] and each text had a different code. Each time we received a text she asked us for the code ( photo attached ). As we were giving her the codes we were logged on to our account and do not notice any strange activity. Then we were sent back to the login screen and could no longer log into our account, and when questioned we were told that she needed to do a master reset. She reassured us that she was taking care of everything and we would be able to get into our account once the call was ended. Once the call was ended we were able to login to our account and noticed a purchase at [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] in Florida for {$2200.00}. The purchase was tried 3 times and returned twice. Also, there was a transfer of {$1400.00} from our saving to our checking, and this transfer was done by this person who was pretending to be a BofA senior fraud agent. 
Right away we called BofA at [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] and spoke to a gentleman named [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]. He was very helpful. We asked if someone from the BofA fraud department had just called us. He said yes, there was some activity. He asked if the lady that had spoken with us was [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]. We said we were not sure. He said she was a senior fraud agent and worked for BofA and she had flagged our account. He told us that activity on our account looked strange and that there had been no new debit card order on our account. He wanted to cancel the compromised card and issue a new card right away. [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] also stated that since the previous activity ( fraudulent activity ) was done by a senior fraud agent he needed to get approval from his supervisor. He did get the approval from his supervisor to basically lock down our account. He advised us to go into the bank branch first thing in the morning. During this same call we were advised that our daily limit had been raised ( to allow fraudulent purchases ). [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] also filed a complaint in our behalf regarding the activities of the senior agent who called us ( [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] ). Later ( at the bank branch ) we did find out the fraudulent  people used the BofA employees credentials as well as another agent ( [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] ) to make the [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] purchase, raise our limit, and transfer money form saving. While the Branch Manager was on the phone with a BofA online presence technician in New York, she told here that this had happened with two other customers from that branch in [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] in last two months, the same two BofA employee credentials had been used for fraud. 
First thing Friday morning we checked our account and there had not been any other activity and we called [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] to make sure that our claim was in process. We did talk to two agents [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] and [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] and we did setup a verbal password for our account, were given a reference number for the bank branch, and were advised to get to the bank as soon as we could to completely close out the checking and saving accounts. 
XX/XX/2019 at [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ], we were at the branch closing our accounts and opening new accounts. The agent did not use the reference number we were given because she had never used one before. Instead she made a phone call to someone that told her what needed to be done. The agent reassured us that the accounts would not be linked to the old accounts and there would not be any more activity. The agent told us that they did not want anything linking the old and new accounts because they did not know if the frauds still had access to our accounts. However, that is not the case and all accounts are still linked to us. On XX/XX/19, I did receive an email notifying me that my old account was overdrawn. Also, all bill pay information, alerts, and cash back deals were transferred to the new account. 
When we closed out our account we were given a closing statement that was incorrect. The statements did not list the purchase at [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] and the transfer date for the {$1400.00} was wrong. However, we did not notice the mistakes until later on Saturday. We had an appoint scheduled for Monday at the bank branch with the same agent, so we would get the corrected statements on Monday. On Monday, when I requested the correct statements I was told by the agent that she could not see the account or get me the needed paperwork. I told them that I need the correct statements and the agent went and talked with her supervisor. Her supervisor was able to provide me with a statement that shows the purchase and the transfer. However, the other two charges that were declined were not listed on either statement. I have attached photos of both statements. 
Monday, XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] was the last time that we met with the bank branch and they were not very helpful, and it seemed that we were more of a nuisance instead of customers. Since then we have called [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] to check our claim status and take care of a direct deposit issue. [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] was very helpful, and he filled a complaint regarding our experience at the branch on Monday. 
We were told that we would have to wait until XX/XX/2019 ( 14 days after the fraudulent activity took place ) for a decision to refund us the {$2200.00} or not. And it may take up to 90 days for the investigation to be completed. [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] ( the first real employee we talked with ), said that the Bank would most likely return our money and credit us the amount on the [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]. We feel that because the two banks employees credentials were compromised and not protected by the bank that this allowed the criminals to act as BofA employees is not our fault and we should have had the refund immediately. And as customers of BofA for that last 21 years, if bank management knew about the stolen credentials that customers should have been warned. We were told multiple times that we would be contacted by a member of the BofA fraud department to file a complaint with BofA and have not heard from anyone.','BANK OF AMERICA, NATIONAL ASSOCIATION',2.0,4.0,'the individual identified herself as a bank of America employee. she explained that one of our debit cards had been compromised. she asked us if we made a purchase at a');
INSERT INTO "reclamations" VALUES(225,'01/11/2019','Credit card or prepaid card','General-purpose credit card or charge card','Problem with a purchase shown on your statement','I read your response [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] .... you are essentially saying 2/3 cards that I said were stolen are written off and you can not find me responsible. I reported all these cards stolen the same day. You understand by saying what you''re saying you are admitting I had credit cards that were fraudulently used on the same day/night and you''re trying to worm your way out of the [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]  charge. I assume you are attempting to hold me responsible for the [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]  charge. In order to hold me responsible you need to prove I was present at the store. Please get a video from the [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]  store of me purchasing the [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] product and I will pay in full immediately. I like how you said you wouldn''t respond guess what you''re gon na respond again, every 15 days Im gunna be writing you a letter until you make this right. Eventually your company is gunna end up paying {$12000.00} + on just responding to me. Do the right thing give me my stolen money back. No jury of my peers is going to side with your company without concrete evidence. I however have ample evidence I was not there at the time I.E. Clock in records of me clocking in at [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] ( PT ), employees saying I was at work, the fact that the charges took place 2 hours away from where I worked, the fact the person who used my phone ( you admit ) sounds different than me a failed my password and did not know my social security number, the fact you told me to drive to the [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] store and not to worry because you spoke to the security team and let them know to give me my money back. Way to many facts are against you please do the right thing you will lose in court and I will make sure the case gets seen by all government agencies. {$12000.00} is not going to be worth a Multi-million dollar fine for attempting to rob your customers. Do the right thing give me my stolen money back.','CITIBANK, N.A.',2.0,4.0,'you are saying 2/3 cards that I said were stolen are written off. you are saying you are admitting you had credit cards that were fraudulently used on the same day/night ');
