 who told me if you were a [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] customer they no longer had my information. I called Experian XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] and explained to them the situation and asked them how could they verify when the company does not have my information? The representative told me that they send the dispute out and that when the creditor responds that the account is verified, that is what they report. This can not be what the FCRA meant by an investigation period. The representative advised me to call the CFPB or my Attorney General or if i could get it in writing that the creditors no longer had my information would do the trick. I called the Attorney Generals office but it was a brick wall so I called [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] again to try and get the no information letter. I spoke with a representative named [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] and she looked very hard and to my surprise said she found it. She told me the Date of first Delinquency was XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] and that the account was 136 days delinquent. I asked for a email of the document but they did not send emails to customers so i pleaded that she save that information so that it would be easy to find. She put in a request that a letter be sent out. XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]  fell off of my [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] leaving the 4 [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]  accounts. Experian 6 [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] and 4 [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] accounts remaining. XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] I called [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] to see if the letter had be approved for sending or had been sent but nothing. I spoke with a [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]  and she could not find the DOFD so I asked if she could send a letter saying they do not have it and she made the request. Called [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] to see about the letter. The representative said it had been sent out. I received the letter XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] and it had nothing to do with the Date of first Delinquency or that they no longer had the information. they sent me bills from XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] along with account numbers of all the accounts [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]/ [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]  . I called that day but no help','Experian Information Solutions Inc.',2.0,2.0,'dispute letters sent to both [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] and Experian. dispute letters sent to both [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] and');
INSERT INTO "reclamations" VALUES(1554,'04/10/2018','Credit card or prepaid card','General-purpose credit card or charge card','Problem with a purchase shown on your statement','Dear Sir or Madam, Please help me. I am a victim of identity theft, and I am being held responsible by Discover Card for {$5300.00} in purchases that I absolutely did not make. The fraud dispute process has failed. I have been fighting with Discover Card since XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ], but every time I resubmit my claim, I get rejected with no explanation. When I call, I am told that I can not speak directly with a member of the fraud resolution team because they " do not accept phone calls. '''' I want to exercise my rights under the Fair Credit Billing Act ( FCBA ). I first notified Discover Card of the fraudulent activity on XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]. The charges were credited from my account, beginning on XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]. I received correspondence from Discover that all unauthorized charges have been credited on my account. 

But on XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ], I received a letter from Discover stating that they changed their minds, and that I was responsible for all charges. They placed all credited fraud charges back on my account! 

This is a violation of the dispute settlement procedures provided by the FCBA ( 161, sections A and B ). The FCBA settlement procedures apply to fraud or unauthorized charges, which I am disputing. Federal law states Discover must resolve the dispute within two billing cycles ( but not more than 90 days ) after receiving my notification of disputed charges. Discover failed to meet this timeline, and the law clearly states that any creditor who fails to follow the settlement procedure may not collect the amount in dispute, or any related finance charges even if the creditor finds the charges to be valid. 

My dispute was initiated in the XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] to XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] billing cycle. The dispute was not resolved within the next 2 billing cycles of XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] to XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] and XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] to XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]. The disputed charges did not reappear back on our account until the [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] billing cycle of XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] to XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]. Therefore, even if Discover believes these charges to be valid, ( which they absolutely are not ), under law, we are not responsible to pay the charges. 

I am attaching proof that the dispute began in the XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] billing cycle, and the dispute was not resolved until 3 billing cycles later, in the XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] billing cycle. 

I am also attaching the overwhelming evidence proving that the fraud charges were not authorized by me or anyone in my family. 

Thank you!','DISCOVER BANK',2.0,4.0,'a fraud dispute process has failed. Discover card has credited all unauthorized charges to my account. the dispute process has failed.');
