INSERT INTO "reclamations" VALUES(1986,'01/24/2018','Credit card or prepaid card','General-purpose credit card or charge card','Problem with a purchase shown on your statement','I filed a dispute for $[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] of a total $[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] we paid a merchant [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] for a car rental on our Chase Mastercard. [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] rented us a vehicle they knew to have a faulty safety system that caused us damages far in excess of the disputed amount. [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] also failed to provide roadside assistance they''d promised. We repeatedly sent both [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] and then Chase extensive documentation that supported our dispute. Yet Chase twice refused to give us a permanent credit only because the merchant denied our claim without any basis whatsoever.

Chase only went through the motions of supporting our dispute instead of pursuing the valid claim based on the ample evidence we''d sent both Chase and [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]. Furthermore, in the two letters Chase sent us denying our dispute there was no evidence whatsoever that Chase presented or argued any of the evidence we''d provided them to the merchant.

Here are more details in my letter to Chase following their first denial of our dispute that points out a number of errors made by Chase and shows a lack of diligence by Chase.

From: [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] 
Subject: ARN: [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]
Date: XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] at [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] EST
To: [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]

To:	[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ], Customer Service Specialist ([COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] Ext [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ])
	Chase Cardmember Service (Fax: [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ])

Dear [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]:

Two days ago I received your letter of XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] regarding my dispute of $[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] of the total $[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] I paid to [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]. Yesterday I left you a voicemail to call me but Ive not gotten any reply.

Your letter contains errors that are puzzling given that Id already successfully faxed Chase the details you requested on XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]. On that date I faxed a complete record of my correspondence with [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]. I am resending that information plus subsequent correspondence with [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] headquarters in [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] through XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ].

The second error in your letter is that I claimed that the dispute was based on flat tires. That is untrue. I never made such a claim.

In fact if you read the record of correspondence, youll see that the $[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] is a billing error by [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] because they [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] was in breach of contract for the car rental in two respects. Furthermore [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] failures to provide the services contracted resulted in damages we incurred far in excess of the entire $[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] total we paid them.

First, [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] rented us a vehicle with an important safety system in inoperative condition. Given [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]  expertise in the car rental business [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] should have seen that safety system fault indicator and not rented us that vehicle. That inoperative safety system ultimately endangered our lives when we had two flat tires and the system failed to alert us of the low tire pressures while we were driving. The inoperative safety system rented to us by [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] resulted in a loss of control of the vehicle from which I barely managed to avert an accident by immediately pulling into the roadside entrance of a business that was closed for the day. Had the tire pressure monitoring system been operative, it would have given me much more time to exit the highway into a safer location such as a service station where the vehicle could have been serviced much more readily.

The second breach of contract by [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] was that [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] failed to provide roadside assistance they promised in writing. This second breach of contract resulted in our being stranded roadside as night fell. We had to find assistance on our own, which we did with the help of local good Samaritans. And [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]  specifically refused to provide a replacement vehicle until the next day, something that was not feasible given the location of the car blocking the business entrance as a direct result of the inoperative tire pressure monitoring system they rented us. So at our own expense, we replaced both tires and had to continue driving the vehicle for the rest of the trip very cautiously.','JPMORGAN CHASE & CO.',2.0,2.0,'a merchant rented a vehicle they knew to have a faulty safety system. the merchant denied our claim without any basis whatsoever. in the two letters Chase sent us ');
