INSERT INTO "reclamations" VALUES(2516,'10/10/2017','Checking or savings account','Checking account','Closing an account','Our small not-for-profit organization setup a small business account with Bank of America ( BOA ). BOA has charged a $ [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]  monthly service fee deduction to the account and mailing a monthly statement. Otherwise, there has been no activity on the account, no deposits, electronic fund transfers or deductions ( other than Bank of America ''s monthly fees ). Due to circumstances at the time, the account was established in a state different from the location of the not-for-profit home office ( basically an 8 hour drive away ). When we called BOA to close the account, we were told that BOA has designated the account as dormant and that we can not close the account and have the funds returned. We were told that to close the account, we would have to physically go to a Bank of America center and make a deposit in order to reactivate the account. Then, we can close the account and get our remaining funds back. 

Basically, this means that BOA can choose to classify your account as dormant even though BOA is making monthly transactions against your account, i.e., making monthly deductions for service fees and mailing out statements. However, the account holder ( you ) can not close the account to get back any remaining funds from the account unless you deposit more funds into the account. Essentially, BOA is holding your funds hostage even though you have a positive balance and monthly transactions are occurring, albeit service fees charged by the bank. 

So, what happens when the funds go into a negative balance, due to BOA ''s monthly fees? You, as the account holder, will owe them money. To avoid having a negative balance is the primary reason that we were attempting to close the account by phone so that the account would not reach a negative balance and we could retrieve any remaining funds in our account.','BANK OF AMERICA, NATIONAL ASSOCIATION',2.0,2.0,'account was established in a state different from the location of the not-for-profit home office. the account holder can not close the account to get back any remaining funds.');
