INSERT INTO "reclamations" VALUES(4226,'10/04/2016','Student loan','Non-federal student loan','Getting a loan','XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] Hello! I am a student who attended [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] ( [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] ) and was forced to take out EdOp loans as a result of the school being overpriced. [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] has since been found to have conducted fraudulent practices involving the students who studied there. One of these practices involves the EdOp loans. EdOp is a private loan that [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] forces students to take out to pay for schooling. Students are even told that they must drop out of class if they do n''t accept the EdOp loan. I was told that I can not attend school if I do n''t take the loan because Financial Aid does n''t cover the high cost of the for profit school. The interest rate for EdOp was [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] % when I attended and only should have legally been [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] % according to [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]. [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] Attorney General, [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] is currently in lawsuits against [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] and is working on EdOp. In the last court case, the Attorney General was asking restitution to reimburse students for this EdOP loan as well as Federal loans. [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] has also had a lawsuit against them in which they paid out {$400000.00} to a former [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ], [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] because she was a whistleblower. She was the one who brought these illegal, deceptive, fraudulent activities to the courts attention. This EdOp loan is misleading, unfair, deceptive, manipulative and fraudulent for many reasons. However, one of the reasons is that EdOp is owned by the [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] family who also owns [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] schools and they also have their own collection agency ( [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] ) which shared the same address. Any school that owns a debt agency and a loan agency which they refer students to is likely expecting students to default and not pay their loans. On the promissory note as far as I can remember, it does n''t say that the loan gets referred to collections after [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] months of default but that is what happens. This is a loan with predatory terms and the owners profited off of it. 
" For instance, upon graduating or withdrawing from school, borrowers immediately go into default after 30 days without payment. Other reasons for default include : failing to notify the lender of a name change within 10 days, or upon death of the borrower or co-signer. If a borrower defaults, the student ''s transcripts will also be held until the bill is paid. 
The mailing address listed on the EdOp Loan website also happens to be the same mailing address [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ], a debt collection agency. This happenstance could imply that these loans, which are made to high risk borrowers, are not expected to be paid on time. '''' [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ], [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ], ( XX/XX/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] ) [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] Currently, [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] campuses are closing because they have been found fraudulent and misleading. EdOp is currently putting students into collection because they are the creditor but they are trying to sell their debt so that students remain responsible for it so that they can get as much money as possible before they go bankrupt from their illegal practices. 
I am asking you for help in dismissing or discharging this loan for its manipulative, deceptive and unfair practices. My EdOp loan is approximately {$22000.00} which does n''t include what I have made in payments. So to reiterate, [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ], [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] and [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] are the owners of [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] and [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ], they are also the owners of the EdOp loans which I was forced to accept. They are also the owners of the collection agency [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] who they refer the EdOp collections to when students default. Please, I am asking for my loans to be discharged so that I may seek a new degree because my degree from this institution ( [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] ) is now rendered useless because they were found fraudulent and are no','Educational Opportunity',2.0,4.0,'[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] has been found to have conducted fraudulent practices involving students who studied there. one of these practices involves the loans. students are even');
INSERT INTO "reclamations" VALUES(4227,'10/04/2016','Debt collection','Credit card','Improper contact or sharing of info','On about a monthly or more frequent basis I am receiving calls for someone else ''s debt. Based on information revealed on the first call I am aware that the debt belongs to a ex-step daughter whose first name is similar ( [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] vs. [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] ) The caller revealed they were collecting on her past due [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] card. I have explained this to the collector on the phone and asked for my number to be deleted from their database. I have confirmed my DOB, SSN and legal name, which is clearly not a match to their debtor. I have been promised my phone number, name and other confidential information would be deleted but no action has been taken. Then when I again explained they were calling the wrong person and asked to speak with a manager, they hung up the phone. I continue to receive calls and now the " offer '''' is to reduce the rate on my credit cards. I have no credit cards but the person they are trying to collect upon does. I block the number they use from the auto dialing system and then another number comes in several weeks later. When I have returned a call, almost always a non-native [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] speaker is on the phone. I can not get them to stop calling me. ( Please bear in mind I have no debt in collection. I have several unpaid tax liens in noncollectable status at this time. The calls I am receiving are regarding credit card debt of which I have no cards and no balances owed.','STONELEIGH RECOVERY ASSOCIATES, LLC',2.0,2.0,'caller revealed she was collecting on her past due card. she asked for my number to be deleted from their database. the caller has confirmed her name, DOB and legal name');
INSERT INTO "reclamations" VALUES(4228,'10/04/2016','Credit card',NULL,'Rewards','I have been a loyal American Express card holder since [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] and have excellent credit. I have always appreciated the high level of customer service that your company provides to its customers. However, recently my Membership Rewards ( R ) Points on my Platinum card were abruptly removed from my account without any warning or contact of any kind from American Express. In my years of interaction with American Express I have never experienced this type of treatment. 

On [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ], I was approved for the [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] Membership Rewards ( R ) Points for the Platinum card after spending {$3000.00}. Once I received my card I began using it and eventually reached a spending threshold of {$3000.00} which awarded me [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] Membership Rewards ( R ) Points, as noted in the terms and conditions of the Platinum card. Then, on [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ], [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] I was charged a {$450.00} annual fee. I continued to use the card and currently have no past due balance. I have spent a total of {$4800.00} which has earned me [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] Membership Rewards ( R ) Points in my account, which ends in [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]. I met the minimum spending threshold, yet my [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] Membership Rewards ( R ) Points were taken away from my account. 

To better understand why my Membership Rewards ( R ) Points were removed from my account, I chatted online with an American Express representative on [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] at [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] ( reference # [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] ) and was told I would be contacted by phone. I received a voicemail/follow-up phone call on [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]/[COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ]. I then called back the next day and was told that some accounts that were reviewed at the corporate level had had their points taken away. The representative said by " purchasing gift cards I violated their terms and conditions. '''' However, by posting the [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] Membership Rewards ( R ) Points I had assumed I had met all terms and conditions for the sign-up bonus. 

I believe American Express is very devious in the way they have handled this situation. The [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] Membership Rewards ( R ) Points were posted to my account, but were removed once I was outside of the 92 day period to meet the minimum spending. If the [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] Membership Rewards ( R ) Points had never been posted to my account I would have kept spending on the credit card to ensure I met the minimum spending threshold of {$3000.00}. Conversely, if American Express had informed me before my 92 days window had expired that I did not meet the spend requirement because the gift cards did not count, again, I would have spent more on the credit card. Now that I am outside of the 92 day window American Express is claiming I violated terms and I have no recourse to get my [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] Membership Rewards ( R ) Points back. With their new rule of one bonus per credit card per lifetime, this now disqualifies me from ever getting a bonus on the American Express Platinum card. Had I known I would not be getting any bonus and I would never be eligible for a bonus on the Platinum card I would have never applied for this {$450.00} annual fee credit card. 

I have actually visited online forums and have learned there are many others with this Platinum card and have experienced the exact same issue. As well, a friend of mine from another state is also having it happen to him and his wife, so many people -- not just me -- are having their points held up by American Express.','AMERICAN EXPRESS COMPANY',2.0,2.0,'membership Rewards ( R ) Points on my Platinum card were abruptly removed from my account. a customer service representative said that he would be contacted by phone. ');
