INSERT INTO "reclamations" VALUES(5624,'07/21/2015','Student loan','Non-federal student loan','Dealing with my lender or servicer','In 2004 the for-profit college [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] and [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] scammed me. I intentionally misled first by the recruitment department and then by the financial aid department. When given the sales pitch of what the institution offers, the recruiter failed to mention that the institution lacked regional accreditation, which I later found out meant my credits were non-transferrable. To add insult to injury, the financial aid department led me to believe that any unspent loan dollars would carry over to the next semester, which in essence would mean that the maximum loan amounts would not be taken out each semester. It was n''t until I completed the program, that I later found out the maximum loan amounts had been taken each semester ... even the semesters I had not spent the entire loan amount and once the recruiter resigned from his position, he later called everyone he recruited to inform them of the lack of accreditation. What confused me most was during the exit interview when I was informed that my loan amounts exceeded the program cost. When I asked how that was possible even with me receiving [COMPTE_ANONYMISÉ] grants? I was told to be grateful for what I owed because some students have left the exit interviews in tears once they were told what they owed and the exit interviewer also explained that they were told by other students that financial aid had provided them with misleading information as well, but still I was not given an answer as to how this matter would be resolved. Now here I am years later required to re-pay student loans on a degree I could not utilize, forced to start my academic studies completely over as a new student because employers deemed my degree worthless and the credits are n''t recognized by the regionally accredited institutions. Any assistance with resolving this matter would be greatly appreciated.','Navient Solutions, LLC.',2.0,2.0,'in 2004 the for-profit college scammed me. he misled first by the recruitment department and then by the financial aid department. he later found out the maximum loan amounts');
